    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d344">

                <pb facs="0533" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/5251: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Munro</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-01-16T19:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 16,
                                1928—7 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>18. Your 23, January 13, 9 a.m. Department is unwilling to pass upon question of
                    Medrano’s eligibility under Nicaraguan constitution for election as vice
                    president. It cannot be placed in the position of approving even inferentially
                    the candidacy of any particular individual. Its interest consists in seeing that
                    a fair and free expression of the popular will is assured and beyond that it is
                    not prepared to go. Its statement as to Chamorro’s ineligibility<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d344-f1"/> was addressed to a special situation and by no means
                    indicates that it will pass upon the qualifications of candidates in
                    general.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Olds</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d344-f1" n="12" xml:id="d344-f1-content">
                <p>See memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State of a conversation with General
                    Emiliano Chamorro, October 22, 1927, <ref target="frus1927v03#pg_367"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1927, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p. 367</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
